docs(adr): ADR-0049 put the ownership question to the operator

Status proposed. The system can say what kind of agent did what, and cannot
say whose agent it was; ownership is inferred from the repository, which is
circumstance rather than authority.

Measured 2026-09-16: doc 02 mentions user, human, owner and operator zero
times each; the grammar in doc 02 section 5 is
spiffe://{trust_domain}/agent/{agent_type}/{task_id}/{run_id}; and
internal/event/canonical.go holds SchemaVersion "2" against no release tag,
so the major that ADR-0045 opened is still uncut.

Both options are argued: a principal in the path, which makes attribution
self-contained at the cost of protected surface 3 in doc 08 section 3 and of
publishing customer identity to a transparency log against ADR-0041's
reasoning; or the grammar unchanged with a separate append-only binding,
which costs the self-contained half and leaves the ownership claim outside
I5. The recommendation is the second, held lightly, with the binding as its
own event type and an explicit line in doc 08 saying the ownership half is
not covered by I5.

The question that decides it is in the ADR: does a third party need to verify
who owned the agent, or only that a specific agent produced a specific change.

